sections.body:
A 'test' without a framework is a script that calls your function, prints the result, and relies on a human reading the output and deciding whether it looks right. That works while a project has one person running tests by hand right after writing the code, and falls apart when any of those conditions stops holding. Nobody reliably re-reads forty lines of printed output after every change to notice that line 23 started printing the wrong thing. A test framework like Jest (or Node's built-in node:test runner, which has a similar describe/it API but uses node:assert instead of expect) replaces 'a human decides if the output looks right' with 'a specific expression is compared against a specific expected value, and the framework decides pass or fail, every time, without anyone reading anything.'

```javascript
// Without a framework: you have to notice if this is wrong
console.log(add(2, 3)); // prints 5, which looks right, but who's checking?

// With Jest: the framework checks for you, on every run
test('adds two numbers', () => {
  expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});
```

That difference between assertion and observation is why automated tests catch regressions that manual testing misses: a test doesn't get bored, doesn't skim, and doesn't forget to check the edge case it checked yesterday.

Jest's two structural building blocks are describe (a named group of related tests) and it or test (a single test case; they're synonyms, with it reading more naturally as a sentence and test as an instruction, so pick one convention per project). Nesting describe blocks groups tests by the unit of code they exercise and produces readable, hierarchical output when a suite runs:

```javascript
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) throw new Error('Cannot divide by zero');
  return a / b;
}

describe('divide', () => {
  it('divides two positive numbers', () => {
    expect(divide(10, 2)).toBe(5);
  });

  it('throws when dividing by zero', () => {
    expect(() => divide(10, 0)).toThrow('Cannot divide by zero');
  });

  describe('with negative numbers', () => {
    it('divides a negative by a positive', () => {
      expect(divide(-10, 2)).toBe(-5);
    });
  });
});
```

Each it block runs as its own isolated unit: Jest catches any thrown error or failed expect inside one it block, marks that test as failed, and moves on to run every other it block. One broken test doesn't hide or block the results of the nine unrelated tests around it, unlike a script of sequential console.log calls, where an uncaught exception on line 10 stops the script from reaching line 40. describe blocks don't contain assertions themselves; they're organizational, and Jest reports failures scoped by describe/it path, so a large suite's output tells you not just 'something broke' but 'divide > with negative numbers > divides a negative by a positive broke,' which matters once a suite has hundreds of tests.

A **unit test** exercises one function or module in isolation, with its dependencies absent or replaced by mocks (covered in the next concept), as opposed to an **integration test**, which exercises several real pieces working together (a route handler that talks to a real, if temporary, database). Day to day, isolation matters because of test independence: a test should pass or fail based only on the code it tests, not on which other tests ran before it or in what order.

A common way independence breaks is a shared, mutable variable that one test modifies and that then leaks into another test when the order or the selection of tests changes:

```javascript
let cart = [];

describe('shopping cart', () => {
  it('starts empty', () => {
    expect(cart).toHaveLength(0);   // passes if run first, fails if run after the next test
  });

  it('adds an item', () => {
    cart.push('apple');
    expect(cart).toHaveLength(1);
  });
});
```

Run top to bottom, this passes, because Jest runs the tests within a file in the order they're defined. But the 'starts empty' test silently depends on 'adds an item' not having run yet. Swap the two tests, run with a random order (jest --randomize, available in recent Jest versions), or use test.concurrent, and 'starts empty' fails. That's an accidental coupling rather than a deliberate one. (Jest's parallel workers run separate test *files*, each with its own copy of module state, so they don't cause this particular failure, but they do make a shared database or file between files a real hazard.) beforeEach(fn) (run before every it in its enclosing describe) and afterEach(fn) (run after every one) exist to reset shared state between tests:

```javascript
let cart;

beforeEach(() => {
  cart = [];   // fresh, empty array before EVERY test, regardless of order
});

it('starts empty', () => {
  expect(cart).toHaveLength(0);
});

it('adds an item', () => {
  cart.push('apple');
  expect(cart).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

Now each test gets a fresh cart, and the two tests can run in either order without affecting each other. beforeAll/afterAll are the once-per-describe-block equivalents, useful for expensive setup (like opening a database connection) that's safe to share read-only across tests, but reach for beforeEach by default; it's the safer choice whenever there's doubt about whether state is mutated.

examples.code:
// stringUtils.js
function slugify(text) {
  return text.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '-');
}

module.exports = { slugify };

// stringUtils.test.js
const { slugify } = require('./stringUtils');

describe('slugify', () => {
  it('lowercases and hyphenates spaces', () => {
    expect(slugify('Hello World')).toBe('hello-world');
  });

  it('trims leading and trailing whitespace', () => {
    expect(slugify('  Hello World  ')).toBe('hello-world');
  });

  it('collapses multiple internal spaces into one hyphen', () => {
    expect(slugify('Hello    World')).toBe('hello-world');
  });
});

function createUser(name) {
  return { name, roles: ['member'], createdAt: null };
}

test('creates a user with default role', () => {
  const user = createUser('Amy');

  // WRONG matcher: fails even though the object looks correct
  // expect(user).toBe({ name: 'Amy', roles: ['member'], createdAt: null });

  // RIGHT matcher: structural comparison
  expect(user).toEqual({ name: 'Amy', roles: ['member'], createdAt: null });
});

