jest.fn() creates a mock function: it does nothing by default (returns undefined) but records every call made to it, including the arguments of each call, how many times it was called, and the order relative to other mock calls. You can configure what it returns and afterward assert on how it was used:

```javascript
const sendEmail = jest.fn();
sendEmail.mockReturnValue('queued');

const result = sendEmail('amy@example.com', 'Welcome!');

expect(result).toBe('queued');
expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith('amy@example.com', 'Welcome!');
expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
```

Real functions don't give you this for free: you're checking not just 'did this produce the right output' but 'did my code call this dependency, with these arguments, this many times.' That matters for functions whose job is to trigger a side effect (send an email, write a log, call an API) rather than return a value you can inspect. For asynchronous dependencies, mockResolvedValue(value) and mockRejectedValue(error) configure a mock to return a Promise that resolves or rejects, standing in for an async database call or API request.

jest.fn() handles one function passed as an argument, but many dependencies are pulled in with require/import inside the module under test rather than passed as a parameter: a database client, an HTTP library, another internal module. jest.mock('./path/to/module') tells Jest to intercept every require/import of that module path within the current test file and substitute an automatically generated mock (every exported function replaced with a jest.fn()) for the real thing. Jest's default Babel transform hoists jest.mock calls above the requires, which is why the mock takes effect even when it's written below an import:

```javascript
// db.js
function getUser(id) {
  // in reality: a real database query
}
module.exports = { getUser };

// userService.js
const db = require('./db');

async function getUserDisplayName(id) {
  const user = await db.getUser(id);
  if (!user) return 'Unknown User';
  return user.name;
}
module.exports = { getUserDisplayName };

// userService.test.js
jest.mock('./db');
const db = require('./db');
const { getUserDisplayName } = require('./userService');

test('returns the user\'s name when found', async () => {
  db.getUser.mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: 'Amy' });
  const name = await getUserDisplayName(1);
  expect(name).toBe('Amy');
});

test('returns a fallback when the user does not exist', async () => {
  db.getUser.mockResolvedValue(null);
  const name = await getUserDisplayName(1);
  expect(name).toBe('Unknown User');
});
```

Notice what this buys you: the second test simulates 'the database found nothing,' which can be awkward to reproduce against a real database (you'd have to guarantee that an id doesn't exist), simply by telling the mock what to return. getUserDisplayName's logic (the branching on whether a user was found) runs for real; only db.getUser's implementation is faked. That's the core discipline of a good unit test: mock the boundary (the dependency), keep the logic under test real.

examples.code:
function processOrder(order, onComplete) {
  const total = order.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
  onComplete(total);
  return total;
}

test('calls onComplete with the computed total', () => {
  const onComplete = jest.fn();
  const order = { items: [{ price: 10 }, { price: 25 }] };

  processOrder(order, onComplete);

  expect(onComplete).toHaveBeenCalledWith(35);
  expect(onComplete).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
});

// paymentGateway.js
async function charge(cardToken, amountCents) {
  // in reality: a real network call to a payment provider
}
module.exports = { charge };

// checkout.js
const paymentGateway = require('./paymentGateway');

async function completeCheckout(cardToken, cart) {
  const total = cart.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.priceCents, 0);
  const result = await paymentGateway.charge(cardToken, total);
  if (result.status !== 'approved') {
    throw new Error('Payment declined');
  }
  return { total, status: result.status };
}
module.exports = { completeCheckout };

// checkout.test.js
jest.mock('./paymentGateway');
const paymentGateway = require('./paymentGateway');
const { completeCheckout } = require('./checkout');

test('throws when the payment is declined', async () => {
  paymentGateway.charge.mockResolvedValue({ status: 'declined' });
  const cart = [{ priceCents: 500 }];

  await expect(completeCheckout('tok_123', cart)).rejects.toThrow('Payment declined');
});

