sections.body:
supertest wraps an Express app object and lets you write requests against it with a chainable API, then assert on the response that comes back (status code, headers, body) as a real HTTP client would see it:

```javascript
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');   // your Express app, NOT already listening

test('GET /health returns 200 and status ok', async () => {
  const response = await request(app).get('/health');

  expect(response.status).toBe(200);
  expect(response.body).toEqual({ status: 'ok' });
});
```

This trips up people used to manual API testing with curl or Postman: you do **not** call app.listen() anywhere in this test, and no fixed port is bound. supertest takes the Express app object and starts an ephemeral, random-port HTTP server for the duration of that one request, then tears it down. Your app's routing and middleware pipeline runs for real (it's an actual HTTP request, with real headers and a real response cycle, not a shortcut that skips Express's routing), but you don't manage a server process, pick a fixed port that might conflict with something else, or remember to shut it down after each test. That's why supertest is said to test 'the app,' not 'a server,' and why these tests are fast and safe to run in parallel without port collisions.

Checking only the happy path doesn't fully test a route. Real routes handle malformed input, missing resources, and unauthorized requests, and each should be asserted with the status code and body shape a real client needs to branch on:

```javascript
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');   // fragment: assumes an app with POST /users and GET /users/:id routes

describe('POST /users', () => {
  it('creates a user and returns 201 with the created resource', async () => {
    const response = await request(app)
      .post('/users')
      .send({ name: 'Amy', email: 'amy@example.com' });

    expect(response.status).toBe(201);
    expect(response.body).toMatchObject({ name: 'Amy', email: 'amy@example.com' });
    expect(response.body.id).toBeDefined();
  });

  it('returns 400 when email is missing', async () => {
    const response = await request(app)
      .post('/users')
      .send({ name: 'Amy' });

    expect(response.status).toBe(400);
    expect(response.body.error).toMatch(/email/i);
  });
});

describe('GET /users/:id', () => {
  it('returns 404 for a user that does not exist', async () => {
    const response = await request(app).get('/users/999999');
    expect(response.status).toBe(404);
  });
});
```

.send(body) attaches a JSON request body (supertest sets the Content-Type header for you when given a plain object). That verifies that a body-parsing middleware like express.json() is registered and working, which no unit test of an isolated handler exercises, since a unit test calling the handler directly passes in a plain JavaScript object and skips parsing the raw request stream. toMatchObject checks that the response body contains at least the given key/value pairs without requiring an exact match, which helps here because the server generates the id and the test can't know it in advance.

examples.code:
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const users = [{ id: 1, name: 'Amy' }];

app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === Number(req.params.id));
  if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
  res.json(user);
});

module.exports = app;   // NOTE: no app.listen() here; that belongs in a separate entry file

// app.test.js
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');

describe('GET /users/:id', () => {
  it('returns the user when found', async () => {
    const response = await request(app).get('/users/1');
    expect(response.status).toBe(200);
    expect(response.body).toEqual({ id: 1, name: 'Amy' });
  });

  it('returns 404 when the user does not exist', async () => {
    const response = await request(app).get('/users/999');
    expect(response.status).toBe(404);
    expect(response.body).toEqual({ error: 'User not found' });
  });
});

const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');

test('POST /echo returns the JSON body that was sent', async () => {
  const response = await request(app)
    .post('/echo')
    .send({ message: 'hello' })
    .set('Content-Type', 'application/json');

  expect(response.status).toBe(200);
  expect(response.body).toEqual({ youSent: { message: 'hello' } });
});

